    "model": [
      {
        "children": [
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "id": "3xeh054j15ei4jl",
                "text": "The Second Law of Thermodynamics: Summary"
              }
            ],
            "id": "lqlj8vpq1j3yjwl",
            "type": "h1"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "text": "Chemical and physical processes have a natural tendency to occur in one direction under certain conditions. A spontaneous process occurs without the need for a continual input of energy from some external source, while a nonspontaneous process requires such. Systems undergoing a spontaneous process may or may not experience a gain or loss of energy, but they will experience a change in the way matter and/or energy is distributed within the system."
              }
            ],
            "id": "9dri1vahrro73fc",
            "type": "p"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "text": "Entropy ("
              },
              {
                "em": true,
                "text": "S"
              },
              {
                "text": ") is a state function that can be related to the number of microstates for a system (the number of ways the system can be arranged) and to the ratio of reversible heat to kelvin temperature. It may be interpreted as a measure of the dispersal or distribution of matter and/or energy in a system, and it is often described as representing the “disorder” of the system."
              }
            ],
            "id": "3l87n5x87k9jmk9",
            "type": "p"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "text": " For a given substance, "
              },
              {
                "em": true,
                "text": "S"
              },
              {
                "sub": true,
                "text": "solid"
              },
              {
                "text": " < "
              },
              {
                "em": true,
                "text": "S"
              },
              {
                "sub": true,
                "text": "liquid"
              },
              {
                "text": " < "
              },
              {
                "em": true,
                "text": "S"
              },
              {
                "sub": true,
                "text": "gas"
              },
              {
                "text": " in a given physical state at a given temperature, entropy is typically greater for heavier atoms or more complex molecules. Entropy increases when a system is heated and when solutions form. Using these guidelines, the sign of entropy changes for some chemical reactions may be reliably predicted."
              }
            ],
            "id": "8f47fl4w8g8w0yk",
            "type": "p"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "text": "The second law of thermodynamics states that a spontaneous process increases the entropy of the universe, "
              },
              {
                "em": true,
                "text": "S"
              },
              {
                "sub": true,
                "text": "univ"
              },
              {
                "text": " > 0. If Δ"
              },
              {
                "em": true,
                "text": "S"
              },
              {
                "sub": true,
                "text": "univ"
              },
              {
                "text": " < 0, the process is nonspontaneous, and if Δ"
              },
              {
                "em": true,
                "text": "S"
              },
              {
                "sub": true,
                "text": "univ"
              },
              {
                "text": " = 0, the system is at equilibrium. "
              }
            ],
            "id": "comutlafgflj6ck",
            "type": "p"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "id": "plhgy7n3kimxvi4",
                "text": "End of Lesson Self-Assessment"
              }
            ],
            "id": "51dp8ym96g0rqdg",
            "type": "h1"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "text": "Before you take the checkpoint, please evaluate your understanding of the learning objectives for the module "
              },
              {
                "em": true,
                "text": "The Second Law of Thermodynamics"
              },
              {
                "text": " and use the My Response link below to ask any questions you might have about the material that you would like the instructor to address. Your questions can be for clarification of the material or further insight. Your self-evaluation and questions may also shape the discussions in class."
              }
            ],
            "id": "30yhn9vvd4fvggv",
            "type": "p"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "text": "Your responses will not be graded, but will be available for your instructor."
              }
            ],
            "id": "3n1y2cta7i7hhlw",
            "type": "p"
